217. What is a virtual thread in Java?

   1. Virtual threads were introduced as a preview feature in Java 19 and became final in Java 21.
   2. They are lightweight threads managed by the JVM rather than directly mapping every task to a dedicated operating-system thread.
   3. They are useful for applications with many blocking I/O operations.
   4. They do not make CPU-intensive operations faster; CPU-bound work still depends on available processors.

